# OJ thought process

This repo is a study partnership, not contest farming. Read [README.md](README.md). Do not start a problem in a chat that is only supposed to land workflow docs.

A solution is right only when Ronald understands the idea **and** the judge returns AC. Do not stop at either one.

## New problem layout

`oj/<judge>/<PROBLEM_ID>/{statement.md,solution.md,<PROBLEM_ID>.cpp}` — keep an existing contest subfolder if one exists. Do not drop a lone `.cpp` at the judge root. Do not migrate old flat files unless asked.

- `statement.md`: full restatement in our own words (I/O, constraints, samples, special cases). Not a summary. Not a wholesale paste.
- `solution.md`: forks, algorithm, tiny example, key insight, non-trivial code notes only. Keep a visual here if it made the idea click. After AC, any optimization worth seeing. Do not rewrite the AC'd code unless asked.
- `<PROBLEM_ID>.cpp`: clear code; do not mimic old `scanf`/golf style; comment only the *why* of non-trivial bits.

## Session loop

1. Create the folder and write `statement.md`. One problem per branch (`oj/<id>`). Never a second problem on that branch.
2. Constraints first, then pause at the interesting fork. Ask which way Ronald would try; continue from that guess.
3. Prove the algorithm on a tiny example before code. If a picture would make it easier (time line, DP table, graph, shrinking range), draw it — Mermaid, compact ASCII, or a small table. Persist the useful ones in `solution.md`. No decoration.
4. Write `solution.md` as thinking settles. Code only after Ronald says go.
5. Never submit to the judge. Wait for Ronald to submit, then ask for the verdict (AC/WA/TLE, plus time and memory if he has them — optional). If not AC, debug the idea before the implementation. Update both files if the idea changes.
6. Close with the insight in `solution.md`.
7. After AC: optimization insights in `solution.md` (time, memory, or anything else that would actually improve it). Do not rewrite the accepted code unless asked.
8. When AC and post-AC are both in: squash-merge the branch into `master`, push `master`, delete the problem branch (local and remote if it exists). No pull requests.

## Hard nos

- Dump a finished solution before walking the thinking.
- Skip “why this, why not that.”
- Narrate obvious code (`read n`, plain loop bounds).
- Skip a visual when a time line, table, or graph would make the idea easier.
- Submit to an online judge, or assume AC without asking Ronald for the result.
- A second problem on the same branch, a pull request, or leaving a finished problem branch around.
